    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The titles of these collages indicate that they were \"arranged according to the laws of chance\". The question\nis what this means. With one single exception, all texts we found\nabout these works suggest that they are \"scatter-pieces\",\nproduced by throwing paper pieces at random on the cardboard, and\nthen fixing them accordingly. (E.g.: Brecht,\nHuelsenbeck, Richter, MoMa,\nMotherwell.) A quick glance on the works themselves is enough to establish\nthat this is extremely implausible. The rectangles are obviously arranged\nin grid-like patterns, and their orientation is aligned with the grid.\nIt is more likely that the paper scraps would have been shuffled like\na deck of cards and then laid out in a grid, one by one, respecting\nthe random order of the pile.\n\n\n\n[Jill Pazereckas, in the 2006 exhibition review \"MoMa\ngoes Dada\", also argues against the usual description of\nthese pieces.]",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "Brecht",
          "href": "http://www.ubu.com/historical/gb/brecht_chance.pdf",
          "external": true
        },
        {
          "text": "MoMa",
          "href": "http://www.moma.org/collection/depts/drawings/blowups/draw_010.html",
          "external": true
        },
        {
          "text": "MoMa goes Dada",
          "href": "http://www.gather.com/viewArticle.jsp?articleId=281474976773051",
          "external": true
        }
      ]
    },
