          "href": "http://www.localghost.de/projects/sl/index.htm",
          "external": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Interactive programs",
      "links": [],
      "level": 5
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In 2002,\nAaron Neugebauer submitted the JavaScript-program PixelPaint\nto the <5k internet-software-contest. PixelPaint is an\ninteractive program which enables its user to specify an image by\nchoosing the colours of the individual cells in a grid.\n\nA pixel-paint program is obviously\nnot the ideal image-construction tool for most purposes: it is not\nparticularly efficient or convenient, nor does it provide a very\ninteresting \"interactive experience\". And for the rare\noccasions that one actually does want to manipulate individual\npixels, that functionality is readily offered by common graphic\ntools such as PhotoShop. Thus, PixelPaint is probably best\nviewed as a primarily conceptual piece.\n\n\n\nIf we do not try to use PixelPaint to construct\nspecific preconceived images, but reflect on the set of its potential\noutcomes, the program itself becomes a symbol –– representing\na seemingly limitless set of possibilities, while paradoxically\nemphasizing the finite constructability of each element and of the\nsequence of all elements.\n\n\n\n[Cf. also: Blinkenpaint\n(2001) by the Blinkenlights Project Group.]",
      "links": [
        {
          "text": "Blinkenpaint",
          "href": "http://www.blinkenlights.de/blinkenpaint.en.html",
          "external": true
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Compiled by Remko Scha, 2003-2006",
      "links": []
    }
